A company that passes every lens is rare. Even my highest-conviction holding today probably fails 2 out of 10. My approach is closer to a score-versus-valuation curve: I’m not looking for greatness at 30x sales, and good enough at 6x sales is often the better bargain.

As long as the business meets my core thesis, I can tolerate some noise.

On the lunch remark, I’d give it very little weight on its own. “Market kya lagta hai?” is ordinary conversation in Indian business, and plenty of excellent operators ask it. What it reveals is worth a look, but I’d judge it on hard records rather than impressions:

Say-do ratio. I’d compare what management said in past transcripts about capacity, timelines, customer wins, cash flows, debt and capex with what actually happened.
Track record. I’d look at results across cycles, not one good meeting.
Annual report. I’d read the related-party note, the treasury and investment schedules, the shareholding pattern and pledges, and the insider-trading disclosures. If company cash is going into equities or group entities, or family trades cluster around announcements, that matters. If those are clean, the remark is noise.
The same test answers the broader question about TV appearances, WhatsApp updates and closeness to sell-side analysts. Frequency of communication doesn’t bother me. What matters is whether the talk is consistent, made public to everyone at once, and later matched by the numbers. A management that is loud on TV and delivers what it says is fine. One whose words and results drift apart will show up in the “say-do” gap, regardless of how often they appear.

I’ll tolerate business imperfections if the price compensates. I won’t tolerate misaligned incentives at any price, because a lower multiple doesn’t protect me if I’m not getting my share of the cash flows.

Everything else, including the meeting itself, is mostly noise. My recent CPHI meetings were a good reminder: how they said it told me more than what they said, or didn’t. But I only trust that read once the numbers confirm it 


